This doesn't make sense, because the Video Element will always follow the Canvas Element...
So using this would adjust it vertically and horizontally:
CSS:
video {
    object-fit: fill;
}
The only plausible explanation is that the video has these borders, that is, they are not the borders of the player (HTML tag for video):
That's exactly why I explained before about the auto-adjustment of the width, if it were like that we would at least see the sides of the video touching the limit of the Canvas

Having the video with the proper width/height is preferable over stretching the video or making changes with it using html/js/css/etc.
(Not sure if the others covered this already - haven't fully checked out the links/posts)
In any case, assuming you are using the normal "Play Video" event command or using the basic video player in MV/MZ, one possibility I can think of is running this code in a script call when playing your video (run the script call before playing the video or during the video being played so you can see it - use DevTools to see the difference if it is working):
JavaScript:
const s     = Graphics._video.style;
s.objectFit = 'cover';
s.minWidth  = '100%';
s.minHeight = '100%';
Hope this helps.

ElvenNeko said:
As you can see, at the start of the video green artifact appears. It is always the same. And it also spreads to all other videos played after. Do you know what that is and if there is a way to get rid of it?
Click to expand...
You could try marking it with css instead and forcing a few other attributes of the video to see if it makes a difference (edit: try removing the 'object-fit: fill' if you still have it):
CSS:
video {
    min-width: 100%;
    min-height: 100%;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
 }
I am assuming the green artifact is probably an issue with stretching the video that is compressed a certain way. Check your settings as well. Is it an mp4 or a webm file?
Stretching it is most likely the cause since we are doing a lot more to the video now. I am also assuming that your video plays fine before it is used in RPG Maker.
Hopefully there aren't any other plugins affecting it.
Currently, this CSS code (and the JS code I gave you) is working fine for my videos - no artifacts or distortion.
Edit: Do note that if you are using my code and also using the "object-fit: fill" code, you might be trying to make it do two things since 'fill' can cause a distortion as well. You will need to remove that part of the css code and see if that helps.

